1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,000
They're like no warships ever seen before.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:08,000
With radical designs and super secret weapons.

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:10,000
These are the ships of the future,

4
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:14,000
built to stalk enemies while hiding behind a cloak of invisibility.

5
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:17,000
Loaded with the most advanced technology,

6
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:19,000
they sail into a new frontier.

7
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:22,000
Now, Stealth and Beyond.

8
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:24,000
See Stealth on Modern Marvels.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,480
de Tandar fighter bombers take off from the Argentine mainland and head toward the British fleet.

10
00:01:07,920 --> 00:01:11,960
Tucked under the wing of each plane is an Exocet AM-39 missile.

11
00:01:11,961 --> 00:01:14,140
There is nothing second-rate about

12
00:01:14,141 --> 00:01:15,037
these weapons.

13
00:01:15,038 --> 00:01:20,420
Fifty miles out from the Sheffield, the Exocets are released and head on a collision

14
00:01:20,421 --> 00:01:22,628
course with the British destroyer.

15
00:01:22,629 --> 00:01:26,720
The missiles skin just above the wavetops as they streak toward

16
00:01:26,721 --> 00:01:29,260
to Sheffield at almost 700 miles an hour.

17
00:01:29,880 --> 00:01:32,380
At this height, they are almost undetectable.

18
00:01:33,860 --> 00:01:36,740
The ship's electronic countermeasures do reveal

19
00:01:36,741 --> 00:01:39,020
that a radar beam has locked onto Sheffield,

20
00:01:39,280 --> 00:01:41,980
but for some reason it is not identified as a missile,

21
00:01:42,320 --> 00:01:44,420
and the ship continues on its mission.

22
00:01:47,840 --> 00:01:50,920
At last, an officer sees the Exocet approaching,

23
00:01:51,500 --> 00:01:52,720
but it is too late.

24
00:01:56,720 --> 00:02:00,354
One of the two missiles slams into the Sheffield.

25
00:02:00,355 --> 00:02:06,363
Its warhead fails to detonate, but the missile's unused fuel sets off an inferno.

26
00:02:06,364 --> 00:02:09,774
21 men die in a vain attempt to save the ship.

27
00:02:09,775 --> 00:02:15,560
Six days later, the charred enlisting hulk of the destroyer will finally sink.

28
00:02:15,561 --> 00:02:23,400
the sheffield disaster is a wake-up call for naval leaders around the world

29
00:02:23,401 --> 00:02:28,520
without a missing piece of the defensive puzzle high-tech ships are sitting ducks

30
00:02:28,521 --> 00:02:33,800
armed with missiles like exocet even a bush league enemy can kill you

31
00:02:33,801 --> 00:02:42,120
the missing piece of the puzzle is stealth engineering the proliferation and the increasing

32
00:02:42,121 --> 00:02:50,120
The amazing sophistication of anti-ship cruise missiles has been one of the big wake-up calls for naval architects and for navies alike.

33
00:02:50,121 --> 00:03:00,120
And if we look at incidents like the loss of HMS Sheffield, we can then see the potency that the modern sea-skimming anti-ship missile provides for a hostile force.

34
00:03:01,120 --> 00:03:06,120
The American Navy and her allies got the message of the Sheffield loud and clear.

35
00:03:06,121 --> 00:03:11,120
Pushed the boundaries of space-age technology to get ships off enemy radar screens.

36
00:03:11,121 --> 00:03:17,480
screens, in short, make ships stealthy or die.

37
00:03:17,481 --> 00:03:22,500
The direct result of this wake-up call is a whole new generation of naval vessels, stealth

38
00:03:22,501 --> 00:03:27,000
ships, which are now in deployment or already deployed at sea.

39
00:03:27,001 --> 00:03:30,880
They are ships that can hide as well as they fight.

40
00:03:30,881 --> 00:03:38,020
They look different and perform different, but all share the same means of survival.

41
00:03:38,021 --> 00:03:42,700
They seek to make themselves invisible to the enemy.

42
00:03:42,701 --> 00:03:47,120
America's Arleigh Burke destroyers have eliminated even the smallest shapes that can attract

43
00:03:47,121 --> 00:03:49,000
missiles.

44
00:03:49,001 --> 00:03:53,940
The Norwegian Shoal races over the water on a cushion of air and with a startling form

45
00:03:53,941 --> 00:03:57,840
that can deflect enemy search radar.

46
00:03:57,841 --> 00:04:03,460
The sleek lines of the Swedish Fisbee class are created not with steel, but with non-magnetic

47
00:04:03,461 --> 00:04:08,460
carbon fiber panels, perfect for avoiding the menace of floating mines.

48
00:04:08,461 --> 00:04:15,060
The German Mako Frigate vents its exhaust under the ship to avoid smokestacks that attract

49
00:04:15,061 --> 00:04:16,700
heat-seeking weapons.

50
00:04:16,701 --> 00:04:22,180
But such ships are only the opening moves in a deadly game of stealth.

51
00:04:22,181 --> 00:04:26,220
The future will bring radically different ideas to the world's navies.

52
00:04:26,221 --> 00:04:30,740
The British Sea Wraith may bathe its hull and decks in water to eliminate heat that

53
00:04:30,741 --> 00:04:31,980
can reveal its position.

54
00:04:32,640 --> 00:04:36,540
And the amazing sea shadow has already shown the American Navy

55
00:04:36,541 --> 00:04:41,060
how future designs may use radically different hull shapes to stay alive.

56
00:04:43,700 --> 00:04:48,320
Modern missiles, mines, and torpedoes have ratcheted up the threat to men of war.

57
00:04:48,880 --> 00:04:52,000
But the challenge of applying stealth technology to ships

58
00:04:52,001 --> 00:04:54,380
is as old as warships themselves.

59
00:04:55,560 --> 00:04:57,080
The sea is a featureless surface.

60
00:04:57,081 --> 00:05:00,680
It's not got terrain, not got hills or valleys.

61
00:05:00,740 --> 00:05:03,380
or forests to hide in our swamps or mountains.

62
00:05:05,340 --> 00:05:08,960
Some early warships used fog banks to stalk their prey.

63
00:05:09,480 --> 00:05:13,220
In the 5th century B.C., a Greek fleet hid behind an island

64
00:05:13,221 --> 00:05:18,080
to successfully spring a trap on a stronger Persian fleet at the Battle of Salamis.

65
00:05:19,880 --> 00:05:24,040
But tricks that worked with weapons such as arrows or smoothbore cannon

66
00:05:24,041 --> 00:05:27,000
don't cut it in a world of modern weapons.

67
00:05:27,001 --> 00:05:30,780
By the time the 20th century rolls around, ships are changing.

68
00:05:31,220 --> 00:05:36,680
We clearly have long-range guns, guns that can fire out to the end of the horizon, in fact, even just over the horizon.

69
00:05:38,900 --> 00:05:48,860
As the world plunged into war in 1914, England and later America turned to camouflage paint schemes to fool the Germans' big guns and U-boats.

70
00:05:48,861 --> 00:05:59,253
Instead of seeing the nice neat outline of a ship, you wanted him to see all sorts of shapes and lines, black and white, so that he wouldn't know quite which way the

71
00:05:59,254 --> 00:06:01,520
ship was going or where the bow was.

72
00:06:01,820 --> 00:06:06,360
He needed that information in order to estimate the speed of the ship to launch his torpedo.

73
00:06:10,720 --> 00:06:16,660
World War I also saw another form of stealth deception by the Allied forces, Q-ships.

74
00:06:16,661 --> 00:06:22,560
These gunboats masqueraded as unarmed merchantmen until a U-boat surfaced to kill.

75
00:06:22,960 --> 00:06:26,660
Then, their hidden deck guns opened fire on the unsuspecting sub.

76
00:06:27,100 --> 00:06:30,320
This early form of stealth sent 11 U-boats to the bottom.

77
00:06:33,360 --> 00:06:39,880
During World War II, camouflage continued to play an important part in disrupting enemy submarine attacks.

78
00:06:39,881 --> 00:06:49,640
But it was an older stealth technique that proved critical to survival of the American fleet at the world's last great naval surface battle, Leyte Gulf.

79
00:06:53,200 --> 00:07:03,260
In October 1944, Japanese battleships crept through the Philippine Straits to pounce on a lightly defended force of small American escort carriers.

80
00:07:04,140 --> 00:07:05,204
Only a few U.S.

81
00:07:05,205 --> 00:07:10,240
destroyers stood between the carriers and the bottom of the Leyte Gulf.

82
00:07:10,920 --> 00:07:16,840
The outnumbered destroyers laid down a smoke screen that would avert disaster for the American ships.

83
00:07:16,841 --> 00:07:23,120
The much weaker American force is able to make a run on the surface over a two-and-a-half-hour engagement,

84
00:07:23,420 --> 00:07:24,840
even though they are radically overmatchable.

85
00:07:25,520 --> 00:07:30,540
A really splendid example of the use of stealth and concealment to protect a force on the surface.

86
00:07:33,260 --> 00:07:40,200
But one huge technological breakthrough during World War II would change forever the nature and importance of stealth tactics.

87
00:07:40,660 --> 00:07:46,180
By the mid-1930s, British scientists had produced the first practical radar system.

88
00:07:47,040 --> 00:07:53,440
By 1939, England had established a chain of radar stations along its coast to defend against German bombers.

89
00:07:53,760 --> 00:07:57,440
And radar was soon being installed on British and American warships.

90
00:07:57,441 --> 00:07:58,365
warships.

91
00:07:58,366 --> 00:08:06,900
When radar came onto the scene in the late 1930s, it totally changed naval warfare.

92
00:08:07,960 --> 00:08:12,740
If you think of a way a warship works, somebody standing on the deck can perhaps see 13 or 14

93
00:08:12,741 --> 00:08:17,206
miles with a pair of binoculars. With radar, you can see 60, 70, 100 miles.

94
00:08:17,207 --> 00:08:18,040
And of course,

95
00:08:18,160 --> 00:08:21,400
later on, when you start putting radar on airplanes, you can see much, much further,

96
00:08:21,401 --> 00:08:22,820
several hundred miles away.

97
00:08:23,680 --> 00:08:28,200
By the 1970s, the Soviet Navy was the largest in the world.

98
00:08:28,700 --> 00:08:30,980
Its ships bristled with radar-guided missiles,

99
00:08:31,520 --> 00:08:35,980
and new technologies were joining radar to offer new threats to ships at sea.

100
00:08:36,540 --> 00:08:40,340
Advanced sonar, magnetic imaging, and infrared detectors

101
00:08:40,341 --> 00:08:45,340
created new and devastating sensors that could direct missiles to targets.

102
00:08:45,341 --> 00:08:54,520
By the 1980s, American naval architects were working on a stealth warship that would counter enemy radar.

103
00:08:54,521 --> 00:09:03,440
The Air Force was already creating stealth designs that would ultimately become the B-2 bomber and the F-117 attack aircraft.

104
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:08,260
Making a 65-foot-long airplane invisible to radar was feasible.

105
00:09:09,820 --> 00:09:12,920
But the Navy's challenge would prove more difficult.

106
00:09:12,921 --> 00:09:20,960
The problems of building a stealthy warship ten times as long and weighing 9,000 tons challenge the imagination of designers.

107
00:09:22,040 --> 00:09:30,040
The lives of future sailors would depend on building new warships that can sail in safety behind the protection of stealth.

108
00:09:31,640 --> 00:09:38,220
The British Type 45 is the first warship to include email and entertainment systems within the mess decks.

109
00:09:38,221 --> 00:09:45,460
It is also the first Royal Navy vessel to include gender-neutral living spaces to accommodate male and female crew members.

110
00:09:46,080 --> 00:09:47,800
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

111
00:09:49,160 --> 00:09:54,160
