112
00:09:57,560 --> 00:10:03,000
As the 1980s passed, the U.S. Navy frantically searched for stealth technology

113
00:10:03,001 --> 00:10:08,720
that could counter the super-sensitive sensors of their Cold War rival, the Soviet Union.

114
00:10:10,100 --> 00:10:15,500
The new ships on the drawing boards would need to be the most powerful and lethal in naval history.

115
00:10:16,420 --> 00:10:21,900
As important, they would need to rely on revolutionary stealth designs to protect them.

116
00:10:21,901 --> 00:10:25,900
J.C.! J.C.! J.C.!

117
00:10:26,120 --> 00:10:32,900
But in 1991, a new Russian revolution changed the rules of the game in naval warfare

118
00:10:32,901 --> 00:10:33,796
overnight.

119
00:10:33,797 --> 00:10:41,500
By the late 90s, much of Russia's Navy lay abandoned or helplessly tied to their peers

120
00:10:41,501 --> 00:10:47,260
for lack of money. The worldwide threat of Russia on the high seas was history.

121
00:10:50,640 --> 00:10:56,140
With the end of the Cold War, the mission of America's Navy would undergo strategic changes.

122
00:10:56,141 --> 00:11:05,340
A dramatic shift away from deep sea military activity has made the stealth race more critical than ever.

123
00:11:05,820 --> 00:11:17,165
What we've seen in the last 15 years is a wholesale shift away from the open ocean blue water standoff that was there between the former Soviet Navy and the US and its

124
00:11:17,166 --> 00:11:17,980
NATO allies.

125
00:11:17,981 --> 00:11:26,120
And what we see now is the whole shift of maritime operations moving into the near land areas or what we call the littoral area.

126
00:11:26,140 --> 00:11:33,920
The term littoral refers to waters bordering coastal regions.

127
00:11:34,620 --> 00:11:40,420
Straits, bays, gulfs, and small seas are now the object of U.S. naval strategy.

128
00:11:42,460 --> 00:11:48,020
These littoral regions may range from half a mile to 100 miles offshore.

129
00:11:48,021 --> 00:11:56,780
The Navy's evolving mission in these shallower inshore areas include air operations, cargo

130
00:11:56,781 --> 00:12:02,780
delivery, gunfire support, mine clearing, and the support of special operations like

131
00:12:02,781 --> 00:12:04,780
SEAL team landings.

132
00:12:04,781 --> 00:12:11,260
The threat of going mano a mano with hundreds of Soviet warships may have vanished.

133
00:12:11,261 --> 00:12:17,100
But pulling off new missions in these littoral waters presents new threats from countries

134
00:12:17,101 --> 00:12:20,700
with even the weakest of navies.

135
00:12:20,701 --> 00:12:25,380
Terrorists in small rubber boats can now threaten our greatest warships,

136
00:12:25,381 --> 00:12:30,920
as evidenced by the attack on the USS Cole in October 2000.

137
00:12:30,921 --> 00:12:33,860
Those countries do have the ability to inflict great damage

138
00:12:33,861 --> 00:12:37,400
through what were unconventional means 20 years ago.

139
00:12:37,401 --> 00:12:41,860
Small boat swarm attacks on our ships, coastal missile sites

140
00:12:41,861 --> 00:12:46,400
with coastal radar sites that, if you operate in the blue water,

141
00:12:46,401 --> 00:12:47,384
cannot reach you.

142
00:12:47,385 --> 00:12:51,380
But now when you have to take the fight into littorals, then you have

143
00:12:51,381 --> 00:12:53,400
to come within range of some of those systems.

144
00:12:56,460 --> 00:13:01,920
Now, even relatively poor nations can afford deadly radar and missile technologies that

145
00:13:01,921 --> 00:13:04,300
dangerously litter global weapons markets.

146
00:13:08,220 --> 00:13:12,284
The obstacles to building truly stealthy ships are well known.

147
00:13:12,285 --> 00:13:14,120
All ships moving through the

148
00:13:14,121 --> 00:13:17,705
water present signals or tracks of their presence.

149
00:13:17,706 --> 00:13:20,860
These tracks are called signatures. The most

150
00:13:20,861 --> 00:13:23,809
obvious signature is the shape of the ship itself.

151
00:13:23,810 --> 00:13:26,700
With nothing to hide behind, this outline is what

152
00:13:26,701 --> 00:13:33,580
is detected by the search beam of radar and is called a ship's radar cross-section or its radar

153
00:13:33,581 --> 00:13:34,351
signature.

154
00:13:34,352 --> 00:13:40,900
What people think of when the word stealth is mentioned is the appearance of the ship

155
00:13:40,901 --> 00:13:44,780
and that's the shaping of the ship to reduce its radar signature.

156
00:13:47,040 --> 00:13:49,440
Minimizing this signature or cross-section

157
00:13:49,441 --> 00:13:53,620
can be the difference between surviving and dying from a missile attack.

158
00:13:55,580 --> 00:13:59,640
Until recently, most warships made inviting targets for radar.

159
00:14:00,100 --> 00:14:02,720
The sides of the hull, the bulkheads and funnels

160
00:14:02,721 --> 00:14:05,220
were all built at 90 degrees to the water,

161
00:14:05,640 --> 00:14:08,500
perfect geometric shapes to bounce a radar beam

162
00:14:08,501 --> 00:14:10,520
directly back to an attacking missile.

163
00:14:10,900 --> 00:14:17,900
Sailors who served in them were unaware, but these ships were actually inviting a deadly attack.

164
00:14:17,901 --> 00:14:23,900
This is the model of the USS Spruance, the lead ship of the DD-963 class.

165
00:14:23,901 --> 00:14:25,804
It was designed in the 70s.

166
00:14:25,805 --> 00:14:34,900
Consequently, you see a lot of examples on this ship that are bad for radar reflectivity and bad for reduced radar cross-section.

167
00:14:34,901 --> 00:14:45,179
They hit the main weather deck at a 90 degree angle, creating a shape all the way along the intersection of the superstructure and the deck that is almost a perfect

168
00:14:45,180 --> 00:14:48,940
The exterior bulkheads run vertically. reflector for radars.

169
00:14:48,941 --> 00:14:54,560
The intakes for the turbines that are below in the engine room are covered by louvers,

170
00:14:54,940 --> 00:14:58,360
which have a design like open slats in the Venetian blind.

171
00:14:59,360 --> 00:15:03,840
That allows radar to get inside of the large air management system of this ship

172
00:15:03,841 --> 00:15:09,440
and reflect back in an almost perfect fashion to any homing missile that's seeking this ship out with radar.

173
00:15:11,900 --> 00:15:16,420
Radar cross-section isn't the only signature that betrays a ship's position.

174
00:15:16,421 --> 00:15:22,420
modern infrared sensors rely not on radar waves but on heat to reveal their

175
00:15:22,421 --> 00:15:29,920
prey. A steel ship on the water because of its engines because of its heating

176
00:15:29,921 --> 00:15:35,920
system all combined to give a thermal signature a heat signature that to that

177
00:15:35,921 --> 00:15:43,240
ship and of course the engines have exhaust you also have reflected heat in

178
00:15:43,241 --> 00:15:49,640
sunlight a metal shape will reflect heat their means of detecting that heat by infrared

179
00:15:52,200 --> 00:15:57,880
one of the trickiest problems to overcome in building a stealthy ship is sound all ships

180
00:15:57,881 --> 00:16:03,960
make noise as they move through the water and noise in warfare can kill water is an excellent

181
00:16:03,961 --> 00:16:09,880
conductor of sound a ship's machinery particularly the propeller shafts running through the ship

182
00:16:09,881 --> 00:16:13,640
send powerful sound waves to those who might be tracking her.

183
00:16:14,240 --> 00:16:16,900
Propellers also broadcast a ship's presence.

184
00:16:17,240 --> 00:16:22,060
The problem with propellers is that when they're going at high speed,

185
00:16:22,240 --> 00:16:23,640
they do what is called cavitate,

186
00:16:23,920 --> 00:16:28,040
which is basically bubbles exploding on the tips of the propellers.

187
00:16:29,680 --> 00:16:31,980
Propellers also create a large wake.

188
00:16:31,981 --> 00:16:35,680
This mass of bubbles floats in the water for miles behind the ship.

189
00:16:36,060 --> 00:16:38,580
They form a pathway that can be spotted by satellite,

190
00:16:38,581 --> 00:16:43,140
and that can also guide a speeding torpedo directly to its target.

191
00:16:46,880 --> 00:16:51,380
All of these signatures combine to transform ships into perfect targets.

192
00:16:51,820 --> 00:16:56,240
As sensors have increased in power and the weapons they control in deadly effectiveness,

193
00:16:56,780 --> 00:16:59,280
new ways to survive at sea are being sought.

194
00:16:59,820 --> 00:17:04,080
In a moment, we will enter a whole new world of maritime technology.

195
00:17:04,081 --> 00:17:10,980
It is a world that is creating new shapes, new ships, and new ideas for a stealthy future at sea.

196
00:17:12,600 --> 00:17:13,300
The U.S.

197
00:17:13,301 --> 00:17:19,700
Navy's Littoral Combat Ship, or LCS, is designed specifically for coastal defense missions.

198
00:17:20,260 --> 00:17:23,920
The LCS is able to operate in less than 13 feet of water,

199
00:17:24,420 --> 00:17:28,220
giving it access to thousands of more ports and coastal waters worldwide.

200
00:17:29,080 --> 00:17:30,820
